# The canonicalization golden vectors are BYTE-LEVEL fixtures. V10 asserts a
# UTF-8 BOM and several assert exact UTF-8 sequences. Any end-of-line conversion
# on checkout would make a fresh clone hash differently from this one, which
# breaks L6's judge-reproduction path in a way that looks like a canonicalizer
# bug. Mark them so no platform touches the bytes.
contracts/golden/canonicalization/** -text

# Contract files are hashed after LF normalization (canonicalization.md sec 4),
# so git may store them however it likes -- but keeping them LF in the working
# copy means hash-contracts.py normalizes a no-op instead of a rewrite.
contracts/** text eol=lf
contracts/golden/canonicalization/** -text

# Shell scripts must be LF in the working copy on every platform. A git hook
# checked out with CRLF fails under Git Bash on Windows -- and it fails by not
# running, which is silent loss of protection rather than a visible error.
*.sh text eol=lf
infra/hooks/pre-commit text eol=lf

# The target agent is HASH-LOCKED at D3 and its freeze must recompute
# identically from a fresh clone. L2 proved on 2026-08-20 that it did not:
# core.autocrlf=true here, `git check-attr` returned `unspecified` for target/**,
# and a scratch clone of the lane branch carried 323 CRLF endings and hashed
# 2060b712f63a6e6c against this checkout's ae3cb4c93f86ad8a.
#
# THE CONTROL IS THE HASHER, NOT THIS LINE. freeze.py normalizes to LF before
# hashing and REFUSES a BOM rather than stripping it, which holds on any clone
# whatever its config. This entry only makes that normalization a no-op here, the
# same arrangement contracts/** already has. If it is ever deleted, nothing
# breaks; if the normalization is ever deleted because someone believed this line
# covered it, the failure returns silently on the judge's machine.
target/** text eol=lf

# The corpus and the benign fixtures are HASH-LOCKED at D5 and the freeze must
# recompute identically from a fresh clone. Same defect L2 proved for target/**
# on 2026-08-20, flagged again by the F1/F2 authoring lane before it could cost
# anything: core.autocrlf=true here, and without this every instance file gets
# CRLF on a fresh checkout.
#
# Instance IDs are safe either way -- they hash the PARSED object, not the bytes.
# This protects the D5 corpus hash, which is taken over files. As with target/**,
# THE CONTROL IS THE HASHER; this only makes its normalization a no-op locally.
corpus/** text eol=lf
fixtures/** text eol=lf
